          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Abraham:</strong> Most people today carry a smart phone, it is a way of communicating with friends, watching and downloading movies and TV series, catching up on news stories, buying things online and booking tickets for a range of different things. It can also be used for playing games and communicating with a variety of other devices and appliances around the home and at work. For some people it is their only point of contact as they no longer have a landline.</p>
<p><strong>Nicole:</strong> There are lots of positives for having a smart phone but there are negatives that we have to consider. As the number of mobile phone users around the world soars mobile networks need to be able to handle the immense demands on data and how quickly they can access services. The opportunity for cybercrime increases as our dependency increases. Mobile malware can target systems due to unsecure usage of URL's over WiFi or other networks. Mobile users are at risk of spam and other cybersecurity attacks. We all know about those bogus SMS messages asking us to click on a link.</p>
<p><strong>Abraham:</strong> Let's start by looking at the hype around 5G fifth generation wireless network. 3G smartphones have download speeds of approximately 2Mbps [megabits per second], 4G is around 3-5Mbps. Currently 5G is at least 3-6 times faster than 4G but could reach up to 100-200Mbps but this will depend on the providers coverage and the areas a person is in. As mobile network generations evolve the speed and efficiency increases and offers a great more opportunities for connectivity and the use of IOT. 5G means gadgets will be more efficient and therefore relied on more but they will also be more opportunities for cyber attacks. There is also a lot of opposition to 5G and conspiracy theories are increasing. This opens opportunities for anti 5G activists and a great deal of false information.</p>
<p><strong>Nicole:</strong> This level of connectivity will be and to some extent is already all around us, imagine fully connected homes, vehicles and smart cities.</p>
<p>With this new connectivity and the use of IOT devices, cyber security is even more essential, as we rely on this technology more, then the need for it to be secure is greater. Allowing devices to connect to the internet opens them up to a number of serious vulnerabilities if they are not protected properly. Application programme interface or API security and public key infrastructure, PKI authentication are a few of the methods that can be used to combat this threat. Imagine if an electric car was hacked what damage could be caused, we have all seen this portrayed in movies but it is a real threat.</p>
<p>Household devices run off the local area networks and should be covered by the router or hub's firewall security, but mobile devices are a different matter. It is all too easy for cyber criminals to clone phones and credit card details by just passing close to an individual.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
